<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=stirrup">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stirrup</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">early 4th century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A stirrup is a light frame or ring hung by a strap from a saddle to hold a rider's foot, first known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> under the Jin dynasty in the early 4th century CE. A tomb figurine of 302 shows a mounting stirrup and one of about 322 a riding pair; stirrups appear to have been in widespread use across China by 477. Central Asian invaders such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> brought them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, where they occur in 7th-century Avar graves in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. By steadying the rider, they changed the tactics of mounted warfare.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stirrup.jpg" alt="Stirrup" width="320" height="345" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Miguel Hermoso Cuesta · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caballo_Haniwa_Guimet_01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The stirrup came late in history, since horses were domesticated in about 4000 BCE and the earliest saddle-like equipment, fringed cloths or pads, was used by Assyrian cavalry around 700 BCE. The earliest hard foot support was a toe loop holding the big toe, used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> late in the 2nd century BCE and possibly as early as 500 BCE. Buddhist carvings at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanchi">Sanchi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> and the Bhaja caves show horsemen with toes slipped under girths, and the archaeologist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-marshall">John Marshall</a> called the Sanchi relief the earliest example of stirrups by some five centuries.</p><p>The solid saddle tree allowed the true stirrup to develop, since without it the rider's weight creates pressure points that make the horse's back sore. Some credit the Sarmatians with the first stirrups.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The earliest extant pair of stirrups was found in the tomb of the Northern Yan noble Feng Sufu, who died in 415, made of mulberry wood gilded with bronze and iron plates. Stirrups are mentioned in Chinese texts by the end of the 4th century, and their appearance coincided with the rise of heavily armoured cavalry, which dominated Chinese warfare until the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> the earliest stirrups come from 5th-century tombs.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, stirrups were adopted more widely by the 8th century, but they were rare in western graves: of 704 eighth-century male burials excavated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> by 1967, only 13 had them. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in the 920s to 980s, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jelling">Jelling</a> kings, many leading men were buried with stirrups, bits and spurs. Accounts of the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> also mention stirrups and saddles in its cavalry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The stirrup has been called one of the most significant inventions in the history of warfare before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>, the third step in equipment after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> and the saddle. A rider supported by stirrups was less likely to fall and could deliver a blow with the weight and momentum of horse and rider. Some scholars credit it with the birth of feudalism. Others argue that it gives little advantage in shock warfare and mainly lets a rider lean further to either side, so that it was not the reason for the shift from infantry to cavalry. In the Ottoman and Mughal empires the stirrup became a symbol of the ruler's presence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stirrup” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>302 CE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Changsha, China</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Toe loops were used in India by the 2nd century BCE, some credit the Sarmatians, and a single iron stirrup from Mongolia is dated 243–405 CE.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/domestication-of-the-horse">Domestication of the horse<span>c. 2200 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/saddle">Saddle<span>727 to 396 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=stirrup">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
